{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"agent_message","text":"The passing version is 3.6–4.5× faster and bit-exact on all 16 cases. There is still a credible one-launch path: profiling shows the reference uses a fixed 64-deep matrix kernel, whose discrepancy from Triton is caused by the order of the 64 floating-point FMAs. I’m checking that order directly; if it can be reproduced, the intermediate buffer and second launch can both disappear."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport torch,numpy as np,itertools,time\nB,S=1,2048;torch.manual_seed(31);xg=torch.randn(B,S,64,device='cuda');wg=torch.randn(B,64,1,device='cuda');y=(wg.transpose(-2,-1)@xg.transpose(1,2)).squeeze().cpu().numpy();x=xg.squeeze().cpu().numpy();w=wg.squeeze().cpu().numpy()\ndef calc(order):\n z=np.zeros(S,np.float32)\n for k in order:z=np.float32(z.astype(np.float64)+x[:,k].astype(np.float64)*np.float64(w[k]))\n return z\nres=[]\nfor bp in itertools.permutations(range(6)):\n order=[]\n for t in range(64):\n  k=sum(((t>>j)&1)<<bp[j] for j in range(6));order.append(k)\n z=calc(order);d=np.abs(z-y);res.append((np.mean(d),np.mean(z==y),bp,order,np.max(d)))\nfor q in sorted(res)[:20]:print('mean %.3g exact %.4f bits %s max %.3g order %s'% (q[0],q[1],q[2],q[4],q[3][:16]))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
